---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import { profile } from "@/settings";
import BetterIcon from "@/components/ui/BetterIcon.astro";
import List from "@/components/ui/List.astro";
import {RESEARCH_ICONS} from "@/data/researchIcons";

const { research_areas } = profile;
---

<Layout title="Research | Jorge Kamassury">
  <div class="prose max-w-none">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-8">Research Statement</h1>

    <div class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-lg mb-6">
        My research addresses a recurring obstacle in applied machine learning: models are trained on data that is incomplete, noisy, or imperfectly annotated, yet they are expected to operate reliably. I study how learning algorithms behave under such imperfect supervision, and how training, sample selection and evaluation can be designed so that reliability is measured rather than assumed.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg mb-6">
        This line began with neural decoding for short block codes, where the problem is posed in information-theoretic terms, and developed into robust deep learning with noisy labels, where the same concern reappears as a question of which samples a model should trust. A third thread runs through both: a background in mathematical physics and computational modeling that shapes how I formulate problems and read results.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Research Areas</h2>
    <div class="grid gap-8 mb-12">
      {
        research_areas.map((area) => (
          <div class="flex gap-6 items-start">
            <BetterIcon>
              <svg
                xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                fill="none"
                stroke-width="2"
                class="size-6 stroke-accent"
              >
                <path
                  stroke-linecap="round"
                  stroke-linejoin="round"
                  d={RESEARCH_ICONS[area.field as keyof typeof RESEARCH_ICONS]?.path || RESEARCH_ICONS['computer-science'].path}
                />
              </svg>
            </BetterIcon>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">{area.title}</h3>
              <p class="text-base">{area.description}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <List listTitle="Projects" listItems={[
      {
        title: "Robust Learning under Imperfect Supervision — ongoing",
        description: "Uncertainty estimation, learning with noisy labels and semi-supervised methods for reliable deep models. Conducted within GAMA/UFSC under a project coordinated by Danilo Silva. Published outcome: CCT (IEEE Access, 2025)."
      },
      {
        title: "Benchmarking Noisy Label Detection — preprint",
        description: "Systematic comparison of noisy label detection methods under a consistent evaluation protocol. Joint work with H. Pickler and D. Silva; available as a 2025 preprint, not yet peer reviewed."
      },
      {
        title: "Neural Decoding for Short Block Codes — completed",
        description: "Syndrome-based neural decoders for short linear block and BCH codes, including iterative error decimation and optimization under critical communication regimes. Developed during the MSc and published between 2019 and 2022."
      },
      {
        title: "Computational Modeling and Mathematical Physics — ongoing",
        description: "Collaboration with the Exact Sciences research group at IFPA on numerical methods, differential equations and physics-informed modeling, including supervision of undergraduate theses."
      },
      {
        title: "Applied AI for Industrial and Critical Systems — ongoing",
        description: "Computer vision, edge inference and intelligent systems for production and mission-critical environments. Developed at ISI-SE (2024-2026) and currently at Fundação CERTI."
      }
    ]} />

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Emerging Directions</h2>
    <p class="text-lg mb-6">
      Two directions are currently exploratory rather than established. The first is scientific machine learning in a stricter sense — structured neural models that encode physical priors, such as Hamiltonian and physics-informed networks, and the connections between statistical physics and learning dynamics. Work here is in preparation and not yet published. The second is the evaluation and assurance of AI systems: how reliability claims can be supported by systematic testing, traceability and documented evidence. This follows naturally from the robustness work, but I have no published contribution in it so far.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
